      "worked": "Let $M$ be the midpoint of $BC$ and join $OM$. Since $OB=OC=R$, $\\triangle BOC$ is isosceles, so $OM\\perp BC$ and $OM$ bisects $\\angle BOC$.\n\nUsing the supplied circle-angle property, $\\angle BOM=\\tfrac12\\angle BOC=A$. In right triangle $BOM$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\n\\sin A&=\\frac{BM}{R}\\\\\nBM&=R\\sin A\\\\\na&=BC\\\\\na&=2BM\\\\\na&=2R\\sin A\\\\\n\\frac{a}{\\sin A}&=2R.\n\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $\\dfrac{a}{\\sin A}$ is the diameter of the circumcircle. For an obtuse angle $A$, the corresponding right-triangle angle is $180^\\circ-A$; since $\\sin(180^\\circ-A)=\\sin A$, the same result holds. If $A=90^\\circ$, $BC$ is a diameter and the result follows directly.",
